= 1F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Number Theory}

= Solution
{parent=1F}

Write $p=8k+7$. In <Gauss's lemma>, for the multiplier 2, precisely the integers $j$ with $p/4<j\le(p-1)/2$ give residues $2j>p/2$. Their number is
$$
\frac{p-1}{2}-\left\lfloor\frac p4\right\rfloor=(4k+3)-(2k+1)=2k+2.
$$
It is even, so the <Legendre symbol> $(2/p)=1$: **2 is a <quadratic residue> modulo $p$.** For completeness, the sign rule follows by replacing those large residues by their negatives; the resulting absolute residues permute $1,\ldots,(p-1)/2$. Multiplying them and cancelling their nonzero product gives $2^{(p-1)/2}\equiv(-1)^{2k+2}=1\pmod p$, equivalent to the residue assertion by <Euler's criterion>.

Now take the prime $p=2q+1$. The condition $q\equiv3\pmod4$ gives $p\equiv7\pmod8$, so $2^q\equiv1\pmod p$. Thus $p$ divides $2^q-1$. For integers $q>3$, $2^q-1>2q+1$ (true at 4 and preserved on increasing $q$), so this divisor is proper and **$2^q-1$ is composite**. At $q=3$, the divisor is the number itself: $2^3-1=2\cdot3+1=7$. The congruence still holds, but it does not establish compositeness.

= 2F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=2F}

Choose a point of $K$ with both coordinates positive, so its product is positive. The set $K\cap[0,\infty)^2$ is nonempty and compact; the <continuous function> $xy$ therefore attains its maximum there. A maximizer cannot lie on either axis, where the product is zero. It consequently belongs to $K\cap P^+$ and also maximizes the product on that set.

For uniqueness, the function $\Phi(x,y)=\log x+\log y$ is <strictly concave> on the positive quadrant: its <Hessian> is $\operatorname{diag}(-1/x^2,-1/y^2)$, <negative definite>. If two distinct positive points attained the same maximal product, their midpoint would belong to $K\cap P^+$ by <convexity> and <strict concavity> would give a larger value of $\Phi$, hence a larger product. This contradiction proves **existence and uniqueness of the positive product maximizer**. This argument is <positive-product maximization on a convex set>; <compactness> should be applied to the closed quadrant intersection, not directly to the potentially noncompact set $K\cap P^+$.

= 3G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Geometry of Group Actions}

= Solution
{parent=3G}

Here a planar lattice means a <discrete subgroup> of the additive Euclidean plane; full rank is not required. Discreteness at zero gives an $\epsilon>0$ such that distinct lattice points are at least $\epsilon$ apart. Consequently every bounded set contains only finitely many lattice points, by placing disjoint small disks around them. If the group is zero, it has the stated one-generator form with $w=0$.

Otherwise choose a shortest nonzero lattice vector $w_1$. Every lattice point on its line is an integer multiple: subtract the nearest integer multiple of $w_1$ from a putative noninteger multiple to obtain a nonzero shorter vector, a contradiction. This already proves the one-generator case when all lattice points lie on that line.

For the remaining case choose the perpendicular orientation so that some point has positive height above the line of $w_1$. Reduce its parallel component modulo $w_1$ into $[0,|w_1|)$. Among reduced points of positive height there is a least height $d>0$. Indeed, heights tending to zero would give infinitely many points in a bounded strip, contrary to the finiteness just proved; bounded sets also ensure that a positive infimum is attained. Choose $w_2$ at height $d$.

Given any lattice vector $v$, subtract an integer multiple of $w_2$ so that its height lies in $[0,d)$, then reduce its parallel component modulo $w_1$. Minimality of $d$ forces the residual height to be zero, and the one-dimensional argument forces the residual to be an integer multiple of $w_1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\Lambda=\mathbb Zw_1+\mathbb Zw_2,\qquad w_1,w_2\text{ linearly independent}.}
$$
Together with the rank-one and zero cases this proves the <planar discrete-subgroup basis> assertion. Under the alternative convention that a Euclidean lattice must have full rank, only the two-generator case is called a lattice.

= 4G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=4G}

A <digital signature> lets the holder of a <private key> authenticate a message so that anyone with the corresponding trusted <public key> can verify its origin and integrity. The signature does not conceal the message. Public-key certification, a suitable message hash and protection of the signing key are needed for that interpretation.

For the <ElGamal signature scheme>, choose a large prime $p$ and a <primitive root> $g$ modulo $p$. The secret key is $x$, and the <public key> is $(p,g,y)$ with $y=g^x\bmod p$. Represent the message by a hash $m$ modulo $p-1$. Choose a fresh secret nonce $k$ coprime to $p-1$ and set
$$
\boxed{r=g^k\bmod p,\qquad s=k^{-1}(m-xr)\bmod(p-1).}
$$
The signature is $(r,s)$. After checking the prescribed ranges, the verifier accepts when
$$
\boxed{g^m\equiv y^r r^s\pmod p.}
$$
A genuine signature passes because $xr+ks\equiv m\pmod{p-1}$. The security aim is that someone without $x$ cannot efficiently produce a valid signature on a new authenticated message. Nonce secrecy and freshness matter: reuse gives linear congruences relating two message hashes, the nonce and the secret key. Signing unhashed arbitrary messages also permits algebraic forgeries, so the hash is part of the practical scheme.

= 5I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Modelling}

= Solution
{parent=5I}

The independent <Laplace distribution> responses have log likelihood
$$
\ell(\beta,\sigma)=-n\log(2\sigma)-\frac{S_1(\beta)}\sigma.
$$
For every fixed positive $\sigma$, maximizing this expression means minimizing the sum of absolute residuals. Thus **$\widehat\beta$ is a <least absolute deviations> estimate**, not a least-squares estimate. Such a minimizer exists: minimize the distance in the finite-dimensional closed column space of the design matrix. It need not be unique if the data or design allow multiple minimizers.

Write $s=S_1(\widehat\beta)$. For $s>0$, differentiating the profiled log likelihood gives $-n/\sigma+s/\sigma^2=0$. Its unique maximum is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\sigma=\frac{S_1(\widehat\beta)}n.}
$$
If the fitted residual sum is zero, the likelihood instead increases without bound as $\sigma\downarrow0$: there is no joint maximum with the stated positive scale parameter. The displayed estimate then denotes the boundary limit, not an admissible positive-scale MLE.

An ordinary <exponential dispersion family> has log density $[y\theta-b(\theta)]/a(\sigma)+c(y,\sigma)$, so changing the mean changes the log density by an affine function of $y$. For two distinct Laplace locations $\mu_1,\mu_2$, their log-density difference is $(|y-\mu_2|-|y-\mu_1|)/\sigma$, with different slopes on the intervals separated by the two locations. It is not affine in $y$. Equivalently the kink at $y=\mu$ moves with the parameter. **The Laplace location family is not an ordinary <exponential dispersion family>.**

= 6B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=6B}

First-season recruits in year $n+1$ contribute $\alpha\sigma\gamma X_n$. Seeds produced in year $n-1$ that survive two winters, fail first-season germination and germinate in their second season contribute $\beta\sigma^2(1-\alpha)\gamma X_{n-1}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{X_{n+1}=aX_n+bX_{n-1},\quad a=\alpha\sigma\gamma,\quad b=\beta\sigma^2(1-\alpha)\gamma.}
$$
This treats recruitment from older seeds as absent; otherwise the two-season recurrence would need further terms. The <characteristic polynomial> is $r^2-ar-b$. For distinct roots,
$$
\boxed{X_n=A r_+^n+B r_-^n,\qquad r_\pm=\frac{a\pm\sqrt{a^2+4b}}2,}
$$
with constants determined by two initial values. For $b=0,a>0$, the recurrence is first order after the initial step; for $a=b=0$ it gives $X_{n+1}=0$. These describe the zero-root degeneracies without interpreting $0^0$ in the formula.

Since $a,b\ge0$, $|r_-|\le r_+$. The positive root is less than one exactly when $a+b<1$: the <polynomial> is nonpositive at zero, has only one positive root, and its value at one is $1-a-b$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sigma\gamma[\alpha+(1-\alpha)\beta\sigma]<1\ \Longrightarrow\ X_n\longrightarrow0.}
$$
This is the decay condition for the <two-season seed-bank recurrence>. The deterministic model describes expected or averaged abundance; literal eventual integer extinction requires that interpretation. A corresponding branching model with geometrically decaying expected abundance becomes extinct <almost surely>, since the <probability> of any surviving plants is at most the expected number.

= 7E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Dynamical Systems}

= Solution
{parent=7E}

The simultaneous zeros are the origin and the four corners $(\pm1,\pm1)$. The <Jacobian matrix> is
$$
J=\begin{pmatrix}-2xy&1-x^2\\1-y^2&-2xy\end{pmatrix}.
$$
At the origin its <eigenvalues> are $1,-1$, with <eigenvectors> $(1,1),(1,-1)$, so the origin is a <saddle equilibrium> of <Poincare index> $-1$. At a corner $J=-2xyI$. Thus **$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$ are stable nodes; $(1,-1)$ and $(-1,1)$ are unstable nodes**, all of index $+1$. Although the node <eigenvalues> coincide, the equilibria are hyperbolic and their stability follows from their strictly signed real <eigenvalues>.

The lines $x=\pm1$ and $y=\pm1$ are invariant and divide the plane into nine convex regions. A nonconstant periodic trajectory cannot cross any of these lines by uniqueness, and cannot be contained in one of them because one-dimensional autonomous flows have no nonconstant periodic solutions. It must therefore lie inside one region. The central region contains only the origin, whose index is $-1$; all other regions contain no equilibria. But a simple <periodic orbit> has index $+1$, equal to the sum of the enclosed equilibrium indices. Its interior stays in the same convex region, giving a contradiction in either case. **There are no <periodic orbits>.**

Away from the invariant lines, trajectory curves have the <first integral>
$$
\frac{1-x^2}{1-y^2}=C,
$$
since both logarithmic derivatives equal $-2xy$. The diagonal $y=x$ is the unstable <separatrix> of the origin, leading toward the two sinks; $y=-x$ is its stable <separatrix>, coming from the two sources. The diagram shows these separatrices, the invariant lines and the direction field.

\Image[paper-2-phase-plane.png]
{title=Phase portrait with a central saddle, two sources, two sinks and invariant square boundaries}

= 8B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=8B}

Near zero the integrand is asymptotic to $t^{z-1}/2$, and at infinity it decays exponentially. The defining integral therefore converges and is analytic for **$\operatorname{Re}z>0$**, by locally uniform domination, also allowing logarithmic factors for differentiation. Multiplication by the <entire function> $1/\Gamma(z)$ does not enlarge the domain of the unevaluated integral itself.

On $\operatorname{Re}z>1$, subtract the two convergent integrals and use
$$
\frac1{e^t+1}-\frac1{e^t-1}=-\frac2{e^{2t}-1}.
$$
The substitution $s=2t$ gives $I(z)-\zeta(z)=-2^{1-z}\zeta(z)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{I(z)=(1-2^{1-z})\zeta(z),}
$$
first there and then wherever the two analytic continuations agree. The <Riemann zeta function> is <meromorphic> on the plane with its only pole a simple pole at one. The prefactor has a simple zero there, $1-2^{1-z}=(z-1)\log2+O((z-1)^2)$. It cancels the pole, giving $I(1)=\log2$. Elsewhere both factors are <holomorphic>. **The <analytic continuation> of $I$ is entire**; it is the <Dirichlet eta function>.

= 9C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= Solution
{parent=9C}

The <Euler-Lagrange equations> are
$$
\frac d{dt}\left(m\dot r_i+\frac ec A_i\right)=\frac ec\dot r_j\partial_iA_j.
$$
Using time <independence> of the <vector potential> gives $m\ddot r_i=(e/c)\dot r_j(\partial_iA_j-\partial_jA_i)$, which is the component form of
$$
\boxed{m\ddot{\mathbf r}=\frac ec\dot{\mathbf r}\times\mathbf B.}
$$
Taking its scalar product with the velocity shows $\dot T=m\dot{\mathbf r}\cdot\ddot{\mathbf r}=0$: **the <kinetic energy> is constant**, since the magnetic force does no work.

For $\mathbf B=(0,0,F'(x))$, the equations give $m\ddot y=-(e/c)F'(x)\dot x$ and $\ddot z=0$. Thus $mc\dot y+eF(x)=-C$ and $\dot z=v_z$ are constants of motion. Substitution into $2T/m=\dot x^2+\dot y^2+\dot z^2$ yields
$$
\boxed{\dot x^2=\frac{2T}{m}-\frac{(eF(x)+C)^2}{m^2c^2}+D,\qquad D=-v_z^2.}
$$
The constant $D$ in this representation is nonpositive. The gauge choice $\mathbf A=(0,F(x),0)$ also makes the conserved transverse canonical momenta explicit.

= 10A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Cosmology}

= Solution
{parent=10A}

Set $s=a(t)/a(t_{\mathrm{dec}})$. Free propagation in the homogeneous expanding universe redshifts frequencies by $\nu=\nu_{\mathrm{dec}}/s$ and dilutes physical number density by $s^{-3}$. Number conservation in corresponding frequency intervals gives
$$
n_\nu(t)d\nu=s^{-3}n_{\nu_{\mathrm{dec}}}(t_{\mathrm{dec}})d\nu_{\mathrm{dec}},\qquad d\nu_{\mathrm{dec}}=s\,d\nu.
$$
Inserting the initial Planck spectrum, the factor $s^{-2}(s\nu)^2$ becomes $\nu^2$, while its exponent is $hs\nu/(kT_{\mathrm{dec}})$. Hence
$$
\boxed{n_\nu(t)=\frac{8\pi\nu^2}{c^3}\frac1{e^{h\nu/(kT(t))}-1},\qquad
T(t)=\frac{a(t_{\mathrm{dec}})}{a(t)}T_{\mathrm{dec}}.}
$$
The shape is preserved even without collisions maintaining <thermal equilibrium>; it follows from redshift of an initially Planckian distribution.

For the total <photon number density> put $x=h\nu/(kT)$. For the <energy density> include the energy $h\nu$ of each <photon>. Then
$$
\boxed{n=\alpha T^3,\qquad \epsilon=\xi T^4,}
$$
where $\alpha=(8\pi/c^3)(k/h)^3\int_0^\infty x^2/(e^x-1)\,dx$ and $\xi=(8\pi h/c^3)(k/h)^4\int_0^\infty x^3/(e^x-1)\,dx$. Both integrals are finite positive constants. Thus $n\propto a^{-3}$ and $\epsilon\propto a^{-4}$, with the extra power supplied by <photon>-energy redshift.

= 11G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Coding and Cryptography}

= Solution
{parent=11G}

For a <discrete memoryless channel>, its <channel capacity> in bits per use is $C=\max_{P_X}I(X;Y)$. The <Shannon second coding theorem> identifies this with the supremum of rates achievable by arbitrarily long block codes with error <probability> tending to zero: every rate below $C$ is achievable, and no rate above $C$ can have vanishing error. The channel is used independently at each transmission, and coding rate is measured using base-two logarithms.

Let $q$ be the <probability> of using the second input symbol. The second output symbol occurs with <probability> $q/2$, so $H(Y)=h_2(q/2)$. The first input has deterministic output and the second has one bit of conditional output <Shannon entropy>; hence $H(Y\mid X)=q$ and
$$
I(X;Y)=h_2(q/2)-q.
$$
For $0<q<1$, its derivative is $\frac12\log_2[(2-q)/q]-1$, which vanishes at $q=2/5$. <Strict concavity> of <binary entropy> makes this the unique global maximum. Substituting gives
$$
\boxed{C=h_2(1/5)-2/5=\log_2 5-2.}
$$
Thus Shannon's theorem supplies operational coding rates arbitrarily close to this value from below, and rules out reliable transmission above it.

= 12F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Topics in Analysis}

= i
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For fixed $t$, let $B$ have the <binomial distribution> with parameters $m,t$. The <Bernstein polynomial> is $f_m(t)=\mathbb E[f(B/m)]$. Continuity on the compact interval gives <uniform continuity> and a finite bound $M=\|f\|_\infty$.

Given $\epsilon>0$, choose $\delta>0$ so $|f(s)-f(t)|<\epsilon$ whenever $|s-t|<\delta$. Since $\mathbb E[B/m]=t$ and $\operatorname{Var}(B/m)=t(1-t)/m\le1/(4m)$, <Chebyshev's inequality> gives
$$
|f_m(t)-f(t)|\le\epsilon+2M\Pr(|B/m-t|\ge\delta)
\le\epsilon+\frac{M}{2m\delta^2}.
$$
The bound is independent of $t$, including both endpoints. Taking the supremum and then letting $m$ tend to infinity proves **$f_m\to f$ uniformly on $[0,1]$**. This provides the required <polynomial>-approximation theorem with its proof.

= ii
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For each node define the <Lagrange interpolation polynomial>
$$
L_j(t)=\prod_{k\ne j}\frac{t-x_{k,n}}{x_{j,n}-x_{k,n}}.
$$
The nodes are distinct, so this is well defined, has degree $n-1$, and satisfies $L_j(x_{k,n})=\delta_{jk}$. Its square has degree $2n-2$, within the assumed exactness range. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a_{j,n}=I_n(L_j^2)=\int_0^1 L_j(t)^2\,dt>0.}
$$
The strict inequality follows because $L_j$ is not the zero <polynomial>. Exactness for the constant <polynomial> also gives **$\sum_{j=1}^n a_{j,n}=1$**. Thus the quadrature is a positive weighted average; the requested nonnegativity follows in stronger form.

= iii
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Choose a <polynomial> $P$ with $\|f-P\|_\infty<\epsilon$, using part (i). For all sufficiently large $n$, its degree is at most $2n-1$, so $I_n(P)=\int_0^1P$. Positive weights summing to one give $|I_n(f-P)|\le\epsilon$, and $|\int_0^1(f-P)|\le\epsilon$. Hence
$$
\left|I_n(f)-\int_0^1f(t)\,dt\right|\le2\epsilon.
$$
Since $\epsilon$ is arbitrary, **the quadratures converge to the integral of every continuous $f$**. The positivity established in part (ii), not just <polynomial> exactness on its own, controls the approximation error uniformly in $n$.

= 13B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Mathematical Biology}

= Solution
{parent=13B}

The <diffusive flux> is $\mathbf J=-D\nabla C$ by <Fick's first law>. Conservation in any fixed control volume $V$ gives $\frac d{dt}\int_V C=-\int_{\partial V}\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf n+\int_V F$. The <divergence theorem> and arbitrariness of $V$ yield
$$
\boxed{C_t=\nabla\cdot(D\nabla C)+F.}
$$
After the initial point injection, $F=0$ and $D=kC$ with $k>0$, so radial symmetry gives $C_t=k r^{-2}(r^2CC_r)_r$. Total mass is $4\pi\int_0^\infty r^2C\,dr=4\pi M$.

A spreading radius $R$ and typical concentration obey $CR^3\sim M$ and $R^2\sim kCt$. Thus $R^5\sim Mkt$. In the proposed ansatz this gives
$$
\boxed{\alpha=\frac25,\quad\beta=\frac35,\quad\gamma=\frac15,\qquad
C=M^{2/5}(kt)^{-3/5}f(\xi),\quad\xi=\frac r{(Mkt)^{1/5}}.}
$$
Direct substitution, including the time derivative of $\xi$, gives
$$
-\frac35f-\frac15\xi f'=\frac1{\xi^2}(\xi^2ff')'.
$$
Multiplying by $\xi^2$ makes the left side $-(\xi^3f)'/5$. Regularity and zero radial flux at the origin set the integration constant to zero, so $ff'=-\xi f/5$. On the positive region $f'= -\xi/5$. Nonnegativity, compact support and continuity at its edge give
$$
f(\xi)=\frac{(\xi_0^2-\xi^2)_+}{10}.
$$
Its normalization is $1=\int_0^{\xi_0}\xi^2f\,d\xi=\xi_0^5/75$. Therefore the <spherical nonlinear-diffusion source profile> is
$$
\boxed{f(\xi)=\frac{(75^{2/5}-\xi^2)_+}{10},\qquad
r_0(t)=(75Mkt)^{1/5}.}
$$
The concentration and flux vanish at the free boundary, so there is no distributional flux jump despite the derivative kink. The solution satisfies the diffusion equation weakly across that boundary and classically inside its positive region. Its support shrinks to the origin as $t\downarrow0$, and testing against a continuous compactly supported function gives the limit $4\pi M$ times its value at zero. This verifies the prescribed point injection as well as the mass normalization.

= 14B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Further Complex Methods}

= Solution
{parent=14B}

For the Laplace-type contour ansatz, differentiating under the integral and putting $P(t)=t^2-t-2$ gives the differential-equation residual
$$
\int_\gamma e^{zt}\{zP(t)f(t)+(2-t)f(t)\}\,dt.
$$
<Integration by parts> turns this into $[e^{zt}Pf]_{\partial\gamma}+\int_\gamma e^{zt}[(2-t)f-(Pf)']dt$. Thus it vanishes whenever the endpoint contribution vanishes and $(Pf)'=(2-t)f$. The latter condition is $Pf'=3(1-t)f$, solved by
$$
f(t)=\frac1{(t-2)(t+1)^2}
$$
up to a constant multiplier. Closed contours avoiding the poles make the endpoint contribution zero.

Choose separate positively oriented small circles around 2 and $-1$. The <residue theorem> gives, after division by $2\pi i$,
$$
\operatorname{Res}_{t=2}(e^{zt}f)=\frac{e^{2z}}9,\qquad
\operatorname{Res}_{t=-1}(e^{zt}f)=-\frac{(3z+1)e^{-z}}9.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{w(z)=Ae^{2z}+B(3z+1)e^{-z}.}
$$
The <Wronskian> of the two basis functions is $-9ze^z$, nonzero for $z\ne0$. They span the two-dimensional solution space on every regular domain, and both extend entire across the apparent singular point at zero. Their initial values satisfy $w(0)=A+B$ and $w'(0)=2(A+B)$. Thus **every solution has $w'(0)=2w(0)$**, and the requested combination $w(0)=0$, $w'(0)\ne0$ is impossible. No additional logarithmic solution is lost: the two independent functions already span the solution space away from zero.

= 15C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Classical Dynamics}

= a
{parent=15c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

<Hamilton's equations> generate the phase-space vector field $(H_{p_1},\ldots,H_{p_n},-H_{q_1},\ldots,-H_{q_n})$. Its divergence vanishes:
$$
\sum_k\left(\partial_{q_k}H_{p_k}-\partial_{p_k}H_{q_k}\right)=0,
$$
by equality of mixed derivatives. The Jacobian $J(t)$ of the flow obeys $\dot J=(\operatorname{div}V)J=0$ with $J(0)=1$. Consequently **$d\tau=dq_1\cdots dq_n\,dp_1\cdots dp_n$ is preserved** for every time on which the smooth flow exists. This proves <Liouville theorem in Hamiltonian mechanics> directly; explicit time dependence of the <Hamiltonian> would not change the divergence argument.

= b
{parent=15c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a time-independent function $F$, <Hamilton's equations> give
$$
\frac{dF}{dt}=\sum_k(F_{q_k}H_{p_k}-F_{p_k}H_{q_k})=[F,H].
$$
Thus a constant of motion has **$[F,H]=0$**. For $F=\sum_kp_k$ and the given <Hamiltonian>, $[F,H]=-\sum_kV_{q_k}$. Simultaneous translation $q_k\mapsto q_k+s$ leaves every difference $q_i-q_j$, and hence $V$, unchanged. Differentiation with respect to $s$ gives $\sum_kV_{q_k}=0$, so **$[F,H]=0$ also in this example**. This is conservation of total momentum from translational symmetry.

= c
{parent=15c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <canonical transformation> preserves the <symplectic form> and therefore the form of <Hamilton's equations> for arbitrary Hamiltonians. In one degree of freedom,
$$
dQ\wedge dP=(Q_qP_p-Q_pP_q)\,dq\wedge dp=[Q,P],dq\wedge dp.
$$
Thus **the transformation is canonical exactly when $[Q,P]=1$**. Equivalently the chain rule gives $[F,G]_{q,p}=[Q,P]_{q,p}[F,G]_{Q,P}$ for arbitrary transformed functions, so the bracket and <Hamiltonian> dynamics are preserved precisely with this factor one. The condition also makes the Jacobian nonzero and hence supplies local invertibility; a global canonical change of variables additionally requires the stipulated global bijectivity on its domain.

= 16G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Set Theory and Logic}

= Solution
{parent=16G}

A deduction from premises $\Gamma$ in <propositional logic> is a finite sequence of formulas, each an allowed logical axiom instance, a member of $\Gamma$, or obtained from earlier formulas by an inference rule, such as modus ponens. For example a Hilbert system can use the schemas $p\to(q\to p)$, $[p\to(q\to r)]\to[(p\to q)\to(p\to r)]$, and $(\neg q\to\neg p)\to(p\to q)$, with modus ponens as its rule. Its last formula is the conclusion. A finite deduction uses only finitely many premises. The <completeness theorem for propositional logic> says $\Gamma\models v$ implies $\Gamma\vdash v$; together with soundness, syntactic consistency is equivalent to existence of a satisfying <valuation>.

For <compactness>, if $\Gamma$ had no model, completeness would give a deduction of a contradiction. That deduction uses a finite subset of $\Gamma$, which therefore also has no model. Contrapositively, **if every finite subset has a model, so does $\Gamma$**.

Fix the given <valuation> on $Q$ and let $L_v$ be its collection of true literals. Suppose $\{s\}\cup L_v$ were inconsistent. A finite subset of its literals, with conjunction $\ell\in L(Q)$, would satisfy $s\vdash\neg\ell$ by the deduction theorem and classical logic. But $\neg\ell\in U$ whereas the given <valuation> makes $\ell$ true, contradicting that it satisfies all of $U$. Hence **$\{s\}\cup L_v$ is consistent**.

If $U\cup\{\neg t\}$ had a model, restrict that <valuation> to $Q$ and apply the preceding consistency result. Completeness supplies an extension to $P\cup Q$ making $s$ true and preserving those $Q$ values. Combine its $P$ values with the original model's $Q\cup R$ values. Disjointness of $P,Q,R$ makes this a well-defined <valuation> satisfying $s$ and $\neg t$, contradicting soundness of $s\vdash t$. Thus $U\cup\{\neg t\}$ is inconsistent.

<Compactness> now supplies $u_1,\ldots,u_k\in U$ with $u_1,\ldots,u_k\vdash t$. Put $u=\bigwedge_j u_j$. Then
$$
\boxed{s\vdash u,\qquad u\vdash t,\qquad u\in L(Q).}
$$
This proves <propositional interpolation>. The empty conjunction is the truth constant; if the language omits truth and falsehood symbols, use an equivalent tautology when $Q$ is nonempty, or adjoin those constants for the empty-common-language case.

= 17H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Graph Theory}

= Solution
{parent=17H}

Let $R(s,t)$ be the two-colour <clique> Ramsey number. At a vertex of $K_{R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1)}$, either its red neighbourhood has at least $R(s-1,t)$ vertices or its blue neighbourhood has at least $R(s,t-1)$. In the first case a red $(s-1)$-<clique> extends through the vertex, or there is a blue $t$-<clique>; the second case is symmetric. Hence
$$
R(s,t)\le R(s-1,t)+R(s,t-1).
$$
With $R(1,t)=R(s,1)=1$, induction and Pascal's identity give $R(s,t)\le\binom{s+t-2}{s-1}$. Taking $s=t$ proves
$$
\boxed{R(K_t)\le\binom{2t-2}{t-1}.}
$$
The four-vertex graph is the <paw graph>, a triangle with a pendant edge; the copy need not be induced. For the lower bound, colour two disjoint triangles in $K_6$ red and all edges between them blue. Red components have only three vertices and blue is bipartite, so neither colour contains a paw. Thus $R(H)\ge7$.

For the upper bound, $R(3,3)\le6$ guarantees a monochromatic triangle in $K_7$, say red on a vertex set $T$. A red edge from $T$ to any outside vertex immediately supplies a red paw. Otherwise all edges from $T$ to the remaining four vertices are blue. If those four vertices contain a blue edge $ab$, then $a,b$ and any vertex of $T$ form a blue triangle, and another vertex of $T$ joined to $a$ supplies its pendant edge. If they contain no blue edge, they are a red $K_4$, which contains a red paw. Therefore
$$
\boxed{R(H)=7.}
$$

= 18F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Galois Theory}

= Solution
{parent=18F}

An automorphism sends the <primitive root of unity> $\xi_n$ to another <primitive root of unity>, hence uniquely to $\xi_n^a$ with $a\in(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)^*$. Define $\chi(\sigma)=a$. Composition multiplies exponents, and an automorphism with exponent one fixes the generator and thus all of $L$. Therefore **$\chi$ is an injective group homomorphism** and $G$ is abelian.

The extension is finite Galois: it is the <splitting field> of $X^n-1$, whose roots are all present and distinct. Existence of a root of order $n$ in positive characteristic implies that the characteristic does not divide $n$. The <fundamental theorem of Galois theory> identifies an intermediate field with $L^H$. Since $G$ is abelian, $H$ is normal; the normal-subgroup criterion gives **$L^H/K$ Galois**, with group $G/H$.

For a non-surjective nontrivial example, take $K=\mathbb Q(i)$ and $n=8$. Its cyclotomic extension has degree two, and its image is $\{1,5\}$ inside the four-element group $(\mathbb Z/8\mathbb Z)^*$. For $K=\mathbb Q$ and prime $p$, $\Phi_p(X)=1+\cdots+X^{p-1}$ is irreducible: $\Phi_p(X+1)$ is Eisenstein at $p$. Thus the extension has degree $p-1$, equal to the <unit group>'s order, proving surjectivity.

For $n=7$, let $\zeta=\xi_7$. The cyclic <Galois group> of order six has exactly one subgroup of each order dividing six, so there are exactly four fields:
$$
\begin{array}{c|c}
M&\operatorname{Aut}(\mathbb Q(\zeta)/M)\\\hline
\mathbb Q&\{\sigma_a:a=1,2,3,4,5,6\}\\
\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-7})&\{\sigma_1,\sigma_2,\sigma_4\}\\
\mathbb Q(\zeta+\zeta^{-1})&\{\sigma_1,\sigma_6\}\\
\mathbb Q(\zeta)&\{\sigma_1\}
\end{array}
$$
Here $\sigma_a(\zeta)=\zeta^a$. To identify the quadratic field without guessing, put $A=\zeta+\zeta^2+\zeta^4$ and $B=\zeta^3+\zeta^5+\zeta^6$. Multiplication gives $A+B=-1$ and $AB=2$, so $(A-B)^2=-7$. The order-three subgroup fixes $A$, and $A-B\notin\mathbb Q$, proving the quadratic identification. The real field is cubic because complex conjugation is $\sigma_6$, with fixed subgroup of order two.

= 19H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Representation Theory}

= Solution
{parent=19H}

For a complex <irreducible representation>, every central element acts as a scalar by <Schur lemma>. If the representation is faithful, these scalars embed $Z$ in $\mathbb C^*$. Every finite subgroup of $\mathbb C^*$ is cyclic: if its order is $m$, all its elements satisfy $z^m=1$, and the $m$th <roots of unity> form a <cyclic group>. Thus **$Z$ is cyclic**.

The trivial group satisfies the conclusion immediately. Otherwise let $G$ be a finite $p$-group with cyclic centre, and decompose a <faithful representation> as a direct sum of irreducibles using <Maschke's theorem>. The nontrivial cyclic $p$-group $Z$ has a unique subgroup $C$ of order $p$. Some component does not kill $C$, since otherwise the whole representation would kill it. If that component had a nontrivial kernel $N$, the kernel would be a <normal subgroup> and the allowed intersection theorem gives $N\cap Z\ne1$. Every nontrivial subgroup of cyclic $Z$ contains $C$, contradicting the choice of component. **That irreducible component is faithful.**

Finally the <regular representation> is faithful, since left multiplication by a nonidentity element does not fix the basis vector at the identity. A cyclic centre therefore supplies a faithful irreducible component of it. The first argument proves the converse, giving **a finite $p$-group has a faithful irreducible complex representation exactly when its centre is cyclic**.

= 20H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Number Fields}

= a
{parent=20h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Since $10\equiv2\pmod4$, the <ring of integers> is $\mathcal O_K=\mathbb Z[\sqrt{10}]$, with <field norm> $N(x+y\sqrt{10})=x^2-10y^2$. Norms $\pm2$ and $\pm3$ are impossible: modulo five they would require a square congruent to 2 or 3. An <algebraic integer> is a unit exactly when its norm is $\pm1$.

If 2 factored into two nonunits, their absolute norms would both be 2, since $N(2)=4$; this is impossible. The same argument for $N(3)=9$ excludes a factorization of 3. Finally $\epsilon+1=4+\sqrt{10}$ has norm 6, so a nonunit factorization would require absolute norms 2 and 3, also impossible. Hence **2, 3 and $\epsilon+1$ are irreducible**. The conjugate $\overline\epsilon+1$ is irreducible by the same argument.

The equality $6=2\cdot3=(4+\sqrt{10})(4-\sqrt{10})$ gives inequivalent factorizations into irreducibles: no norm-six factor is associated to either 2 or 3, whose norms are 4 and 9. A <principal ideal domain> is a <unique factorization domain>. Consequently **$\mathcal O_K$ is not a <principal ideal domain>**.

= b
{parent=20h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $r=\sqrt{10}$. The ideals are $\mathfrak p_2=(2,r)$, $\mathfrak p_3=(3,r+1)$ and $\overline{\mathfrak p}_3=(3,r-1)$, with residue fields of orders 2, 3 and 3 respectively. Thus their ideal norms are 2, 3 and 3.

The generators of $\mathfrak p_2^2$ are $4,2r,10$. They are multiples of 2, and the integer combination of 4 and 10 giving 2 proves $\mathfrak p_2^2=(2)$. Similarly $\mathfrak p_3\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$ is generated by $9,3r+3,3r-3,r^2-1=9$. The difference of the middle generators is 6; together with 9 this generates 3. Therefore the product is $(3)$.

The ideals $\mathfrak p_2$ and $\mathfrak p_3$ are coprime, so their product equals their intersection. The element $4+r$ belongs to both, hence $(4+r)\subseteq\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3$. Both ideals have norm 6, so equality holds. Likewise $2+r$ belongs to $\mathfrak p_2$ and $\overline{\mathfrak p}_3$, and its norm is $-6$. Thus
$$
\boxed{(2)=\mathfrak p_2^2,\quad(3)=\mathfrak p_3\overline{\mathfrak p}_3,\quad
\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3=(\epsilon+1),\quad
\mathfrak p_2\overline{\mathfrak p}_3=(\epsilon-1).}
$$
The <discriminant> is 40. The <Minkowski bound for ideal classes> in a real quadratic field is $\sqrt{40}/2=\sqrt{10}<4$, so every <ideal class> has an integral representative of norm 1, 2 or 3. The only possible prime ideals with those norms are the ones just listed. Their classes satisfy $[\mathfrak p_2]^2=1$ and $[\mathfrak p_3]=[\overline{\mathfrak p}_3]=[\mathfrak p_2]$. Thus there are at most two classes. The ideal $\mathfrak p_2$ is not principal, since a generator would have norm $\pm2$, already ruled out. Therefore **the <class number> is exactly two**.

= c
{parent=20h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The element $\epsilon=3+\sqrt{10}>1$ has norm $-1$. To prove that it is a <fundamental unit>, let $u=x+y\sqrt{10}$ be a positive real unit with $1<u<\epsilon$. Its conjugate is $\pm1/u$, so $y=(u-\overline u)/(2\sqrt{10})>0$. For norm $-1$, the increasing function $u+1/u$ gives $y<(\epsilon+1/\epsilon)/(2\sqrt{10})=1$; for norm $+1$, $y<(\epsilon-1/\epsilon)/(2\sqrt{10})=3/\sqrt{10}<1$. Either contradicts integer $y$. There is no positive unit between one and $\epsilon$. For any positive unit, multiply by a suitable integer power of $\epsilon$ to place it in $[1,\epsilon)$; it must then equal one. Hence
$$
\boxed{\mathcal O_K^*=\{\pm\epsilon^n:n\in\mathbb Z\}.}
$$
To classify the norm-six elements, a principal ideal generated by such an element has norm 6. Unique prime-ideal factorization and part (b) force it to be either $\mathfrak p_2\mathfrak p_3=(\epsilon+1)$ or $\mathfrak p_2\overline{\mathfrak p}_3=(\epsilon-1)$. Therefore its generator is an associate of one of these two elements. Since their norms are respectively 6 and $-6$, the unit multiplying the first must have even exponent and the unit multiplying the second must have odd exponent. Combining the two cases gives all integer solutions:
$$
\boxed{x+y\sqrt{10}=\pm\epsilon^n(\epsilon+(-1)^n),\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Conversely every displayed element is integral, since $\epsilon$ is a unit, and has norm 6. This proves completeness of the <norm-six equation in the quadratic field of square root ten>.

The PDF's restriction to $n=0,1,2,\ldots$ misses conjugate solutions. In particular $(x,y)=(4,-1)$ satisfies the equation but is not in that restricted family; it is the $n=-1$ member. A nonnegative-index version becomes correct by adding the conjugates of every displayed element, with both signs. The restriction as printed cannot describe all signed integer pairs.

= 21H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Algebraic Topology}

= Solution
{parent=21H}

Use <simplicial homology> with a fixed coefficient group or ring. The <Mayer–Vietoris sequence> is
$$
\cdots\longrightarrow H_{i+1}(X)\xrightarrow{\delta}H_i(A\cap B)
\xrightarrow{\alpha}H_i(A)\oplus H_i(B)
\xrightarrow{\beta}H_i(X)\xrightarrow{\delta}H_{i-1}(A\cap B)\longrightarrow\cdots.
$$
The inclusion maps define $\alpha([z])=([z],-[z])$ and $\beta([a],[b])=[a+b]$. To define $\delta$, split a <simplicial cycle> $c\in C_{i+1}(X)$ as $c=a+b$ with $a$ a chain in $A$ and $b$ in $B$. Since $\partial a=-\partial b$, this boundary lies in $C_i(A\cap B)$, and $\delta[c]=[\partial a]$. At degree zero the unreduced sequence ends with $H_0(A)\oplus H_0(B)\to H_0(X)\to0$.

If $[z]=\delta[c]$, its representative is a boundary in $A$ and the negative of a boundary in $B$, so $\alpha[z]=0$. Thus $\operatorname{im}\delta\subseteq\ker\alpha$.

Conversely if $\alpha[z]=0$, choose $a\in C_{i+1}(A)$ and $b\in C_{i+1}(B)$ with $\partial a=z=\partial b$. The chain $c=a-b$ is a <simplicial cycle> in $X$. Its splitting into the $A$ chain $a$ and $B$ chain $-b$ gives $\delta[c]=[z]$. Hence **$\operatorname{im}\delta=\ker\alpha$**, the requested exactness at $H_i(A\cap B)$.

= 22G
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Linear Analysis}

= Solution
{parent=22G}

Let $B_X$ be the open unit ball. Since $T(X)=\bigcup_{n\ge1}T(nB_X)$ is not meagre in $Y$, at least one closure $\overline{T(nB_X)}$ has nonempty interior. A ball contained in that closure, subtracted from itself, gives a ball about zero contained in $\overline{T(2nB_X)}$. Scaling yields some $\delta>0$ with
$$
B_Y(0,\delta)\subseteq\overline{T(B_X)}.
$$
No completeness of $Y$ is used here; the hypothesis is second category in $Y$, not merely in the relative image topology.

If $\|y\|<\delta/2$, choose $x_1\in\tfrac12B_X$ with $\|y-Tx_1\|<\delta/4$. Then choose $x_2\in\tfrac14B_X$ so the new residual is below $\delta/8$, and continue. The series $\sum_jx_j$ converges in the <Banach space> $X$, has norm less than one, and continuity of $T$ gives $T(\sum_jx_j)=y$. Thus $B_Y(0,\delta/2)\subseteq T(B_X)$.

The linear subspace $T(X)$ contains a ball about zero, so it is all of $Y$: **$T$ is surjective**. Translated and scaled unit-ball images show that every open-set image is open. If $T$ is injective, its inverse is linear and the ball inclusion gives $\|T^{-1}y\|\le(2/\delta)\|y\|$, by scaling and taking a limiting bound. Thus **$T^{-1}$ exists and is bounded**.

For the first nonlinear counterexample use $f(x)=e^x$: its image $(0,\infty)$ is of second category but is not all of $\mathbb R$. For the second use the continuous surjection $f(x)=x+1$ for $x\le-1$, $f(x)=0$ for $-1\le x\le1$, and $f(x)=x-1$ for $x\ge1$. The open interval $(-1/2,1/2)$ maps to the nonopen singleton $\{0\}$. Linearity is essential to the conclusions.

= 23F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Riemann Surfaces}

= Solution
{parent=23F}

Put $b=\operatorname{Im}\tau>0$. On $|\operatorname{Im}z|\le R$, the modulus of the $n$th summand is at most
$$
\exp[-\pi b(n+1/2)^2+2\pi R|n+1/2|].
$$
The resulting Gaussian majorant is summable, independently of the unbounded real part of $z$. The Weierstrass test proves <uniform convergence> on every such strip. Since the summands are entire, locally <uniform convergence> implies **$\psi$ is entire**. It is not identically zero: integrating $\psi(x)e^{-\pi ix}$ over $0\le x\le1$ extracts its nonzero $n=0$ Fourier coefficient.

Logarithmic differentiation of the two supplied transformation laws gives
$$
\boxed{\ell(z+1)=\ell(z),\qquad \ell(z+\tau)=\ell(z)-2\pi i.}
$$
These hold as <meromorphic> identities, with poles at zeros of $\psi$. For a translated fundamental parallelogram with no boundary zeros, opposite edges parallel to $\tau$ cancel by the first identity. The other edge pair contributes $2\pi i$ by the second, so the <argument principle> counts exactly one zero, including multiplicity, in that parallelogram.

Changing the summation index $n\mapsto-n-1$ shows $\psi(-z)=-\psi(z)$, hence $\psi(0)=0$. Quasiperiodicity makes every lattice point a zero. Every generic fundamental parallelogram contains exactly one lattice point, which already accounts for its one zero; varying its position rules out any additional zeros and proves the lattice zeros are simple. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\{z:\psi(z)=0\}=\Lambda=\mathbb Z+\tau\mathbb Z,\quad\text{all zeros simple}.}
$$
In particular the centred parallelogram in the question has no lattice points on its boundary and its unique zero is zero. Using generic translated contours first avoids assuming this boundary fact before determining the zeros.

For the proposed sum, the first period always holds, while $f(z+\tau)-f(z)=-2\pi i\sum_j\lambda_j$. Thus **it is $\Lambda$-periodic exactly when $\sum_j\lambda_j=0$**. Meromorphicity follows from the <meromorphic> logarithmic derivatives. Finally differentiation removes the additive shift in $\ell$, so **$\frac d{dz}[\psi'(z-a)/\psi(z-a)]$ has both periods** for every $a$.

= 24H
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Geometry}

= i
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <minimal surface> has zero <mean curvature> $H=(k_1+k_2)/2$; equivalently its area is stationary under compactly supported normal variations. Its <principal curvatures> satisfy $k_2=-k_1$, so its <Gaussian curvature> is
$$
\boxed{K=k_1k_2=-k_1^2\le0.}
$$
Equality occurs precisely where its <second fundamental form> vanishes.

= ii
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $A=-dN$ be the self-adjoint <shape operator>. In an orthonormal principal basis, $\langle dN(v),dN(w)\rangle=\langle Av,Aw\rangle$, with diagonal coefficients $k_1^2,k_2^2$. The <Gauss map> is conformal with nonzero factor exactly when $k_1^2=k_2^2=\lambda>0$.

If the surface is minimal and has no umbilics, $k_2=-k_1$ and $k_1\ne0$, so this equality holds. Conversely equality of the squares gives either $k_1=k_2$ or $k_1=-k_2$. The first possibility is excluded by the no-umbilic hypothesis. Hence $H=0$, proving **minimality is equivalent to conformality of the <Gauss map> under the stated hypothesis**. The factor is automatically positive because it equates positive definite inner products. The hypothesis is essential: a sphere has conformal <Gauss map> and nonzero <mean curvature>.

= iii
{parent=24h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At a non-planar point of a <minimal surface>, the <shape operator> is not zero. Minimality gives nonzero opposite <principal curvatures>, so $K<0$ nearby and $dN$ is invertible. The <inverse function theorem> makes the <Gauss map> a local diffeomorphism onto a patch of the sphere; part (ii) makes it conformal.

Choose stereographic coordinates $(u,v)$ on that sphere patch. Their metric is $4(du^2+dv^2)/(1+u^2+v^2)^2$. Pulling them back by $N$, the conformal identity $N^*g_{S^2}=\lambda g_S$ gives
$$
g_S=\frac4{\lambda(1+u^2+v^2)^2}(du^2+dv^2).
$$
The positive coefficient shows that **these are local <isothermal coordinates>**. No general isothermal-coordinate existence theorem is needed: the nondegenerate conformal <Gauss map> supplies them explicitly.

= 25J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability and Measure}

= a
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The first <Borel-Cantelli lemma> says that $\sum_n\Pr(A_n)<\infty$ implies $\Pr(A_n\text{ infinitely often})=0$, without an <independence> assumption. Indeed, for every $N$,
$$
\Pr\left(\bigcup_{n\ge N}A_n\right)\le\sum_{n\ge N}\Pr(A_n)\longrightarrow0.
$$
The decreasing intersection of these tail unions is the infinitely-often event; continuity of <probability> from above proves the assertion. The second lemma says that for independent $A_n$, divergence of the <probability> sum implies $\Pr(A_n\text{ infinitely often})=1$.

= b
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

<Convergence in probability> supplies a subsequence $X_{n_k}$ converging <almost surely> to $X$: choose it so $\Pr(|X_{n_k}-X|>2^{-k})<2^{-k}$, then apply the first <Borel-Cantelli lemma>. For each fixed $N$, the tail of this subsequence lies in $\sigma(X_n:n\ge N)$, and its almost sure limit is $X$. Thus $X$ has a version measurable with respect to the completed tail sigma-field of the independent sequence.

By <Kolmogorov's zero-one law>, every event in that tail sigma-field has <probability> zero or one. Consequently $\Pr(X\le r)\in\{0,1\}$ for every rational $r$. Since $X$ is a finite real <random variable>, its distribution function has limits zero and one at the two infinities; monotonicity then identifies a single threshold $c$ with $\Pr(X=c)=1$. Hence **$X$ is <almost surely> constant**. Completion only adds null events and does not alter the zero-one conclusion.

= c
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Under <complete convergence of random variables>, for each integer $k\ge1$ the first <Borel-Cantelli lemma> gives $\Pr(|X_n-X|>1/k\text{ infinitely often})=0$. Intersecting these <probability>-one conclusions over the countably many $k$ shows that every tolerance is eventually satisfied. Therefore **<complete convergence> implies <almost sure convergence>**, without <independence>.

= d
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

<Almost sure convergence> implies <convergence in probability>, so part (b) identifies the limit as a constant $c$ <almost surely>. For each $\epsilon>0$, the events $A_n=\{|X_n-c|>\epsilon\}$ are independent. <Almost sure convergence> gives $\Pr(A_n\text{ infinitely often})=0$. If their <probability> sum diverged, the second <Borel-Cantelli lemma> would instead give <probability> one. Hence the sum is finite for every $\epsilon$, proving **<almost sure convergence> implies <complete convergence> for independent sequences**.

= e
{parent=25j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Let $U$ be one uniform <random variable> on $(0,1)$, and define
$$
\boxed{X_n=\mathbf1_{\{U\le1/n\}}.}
$$
For every $U>0$ the sequence is eventually zero, so $X_n\to0$ <almost surely>. But $\Pr(|X_n|>1/2)=1/n$, whose sum diverges. This sequence is not completely convergent; the exceedance events are nested and dependent.

= 26J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applied Probability}

= a
{parent=26j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For $i\ne j$ the generator entry is $q_{ij}=r_j$. Its diagonal is the negative total departure rate, $q_{ii}=-(R-r_i)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{Q=\mathbf1r^T-RI,\qquad r=(r_1,\ldots,r_s)^T.}
$$
Every row sums to zero, as required for a continuous-time Markov-chain generator.

= b
{parent=26j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

When $R=0$ all rates vanish. Every state is absorbing and **the communicating classes are the $s$ singletons**, all closed. There are no open communicating classes. Every <probability> distribution is stationary in this degenerate case.

= c
{parent=26j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $R>0$, **the chain has one <communicating class> exactly when every $r_j>0$**. Sufficiency follows from the positive direct transition rate between each distinct pair of states. For necessity, if $r_j=0$ and there is another state, no other state can jump to $j$, either directly or as the final step of a path, so $j$ cannot communicate with them. The one-state case also satisfies the condition because $R=r_1>0$. A single class containing the entire state space is automatically closed.

= d
{parent=26j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $S_+=\{j:r_j>0\}$, nonempty since $R>0$. Every pair of distinct states in $S_+$ communicates directly, and no transition leaves $S_+$ for a zero-rate state. Hence **$S_+$ is the unique <closed communicating class>**. Each zero-rate state forms an open singleton: it has total departure rate $R>0$, goes into $S_+$, and can never be revisited. This includes the case that $S_+$ itself is a single absorbing state.

= e
{parent=26j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The stationary equation $\pi Q=0$ reads $r^T-R\pi=0$, because $\pi\mathbf1=1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\pi_j=\frac{r_j}{R}.}
$$
It is the unique stationary law and vanishes on the open classes. For distinct $i,j$, $\pi_iq_{ij}=r_ir_j/R=\pi_jq_{ji}$. Thus **the chain is reversible in its equilibrium law**, including the reducible cases; on the closed class this is the usual reversible irreducible chain. This structure is the <destination-dependent reset chain>.

= f
{parent=26j}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

At a state with positive departure rate $\lambda_i=R-r_i$, the next-jump probabilities are
$$
\boxed{\widehat p_{ij}=\frac{r_j}{R-r_i}\ (j\ne i),\qquad\widehat p_{ii}=0.}
$$
If at least two rates are positive, every state has positive departure rate and the normalizing constant $Z=\sum_i r_i(R-r_i)=R^2-\sum_i r_i^2$ is positive. The jump-chain equilibrium law is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\pi_i=\frac{r_i(R-r_i)}{Z}.}
$$
Indeed $\widehat\pi_i\widehat p_{ij}=r_ir_j/Z$ is symmetric, proving both stationarity and reversibility. Jump observations weight the continuous-time equilibrium by the departure rate, so $\widehat\pi$ is generally different from $\pi$.

If exactly one rate, $r_k$, is positive, state $k$ is absorbing and the denominator $R-r_k$ vanishes. There is no next jump from that state: the genuine embedded jump sequence terminates there. Under the usual extended-chain convention set $\widehat p_{kk}=1$; every other state jumps to $k$ with <probability> one. Its equilibrium law is $\delta_k$ and it is trivially reversible there. The normalized $r_i(R-r_i)$ formula cannot be used in that absorbing case.

= 27I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Statistics}

= i
{parent=27i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The likelihood-ratio statistic for nested parameter spaces is
$$
\Lambda_n=\frac{\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_0}L_n(\theta)}{\sup_{\theta\in\Theta_1}L_n(\theta)},\qquad W_n=-2\log\Lambda_n.
$$
Under the null, <Wilks' theorem> gives $W_n\Rightarrow\chi^2_{d_1-d_0}$ when the full model and null submodel are smooth and identifiable, the true parameter is an interior regular point, <Fisher information> is nonsingular in the identifiable directions, and the usual differentiability and likelihood-asymptotic conditions hold. A level-$\alpha$ test rejects for $W_n$ exceeding the upper $\alpha$ quantile of that chi-square law. The approximation is for large samples; boundary parameters, singularities or nonidentifiability can give a different limiting distribution.

= ii
{parent=27i}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The unrestricted categorical MLE is $\widetilde\pi_j=N_j/n$, so the multinomial combinatorial factor cancels from the likelihood ratio and
$$
W_n=2\sum_jN_j\log\frac{N_j}{n\widehat\pi_j}.
$$
Assume the true cell probabilities are positive and the null has identifiable dimension $d$ with full-rank information. With <probability> tending to one, all $N_j$ are positive. Put $\delta_j=n\widehat\pi_j-N_j$. Regular estimation gives $\delta_j=O_p(\sqrt n)$ and $N_j$ of order $n$. Expanding $-2N_j\log(1+\delta_j/N_j)$ yields
$$
W_n=\sum_j\left[-2\delta_j+\frac{\delta_j^2}{N_j}+O_p(n^{-1/2})\right].
$$
The linear terms cancel exactly because $\sum_j\delta_j=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{W_n=\sum_{j=1}^K\frac{(N_j-n\widehat\pi_j)^2}{N_j}+o_p(1)
\ \Rightarrow\ \chi^2_{K-1-d}.}
$$
The full <probability> simplex has dimension $K-1$, explaining the degrees of freedom. Existence and uniqueness of an MLE alone do not supply all Wilks regularity assumptions; positivity, smoothness and the local rank condition are also needed. The displayed approximation is not a finite-sample prescription for division by a zero observed count.

= 28J
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Stochastic Financial Models}

= Solution
{parent=28J}

An <arbitrage> is a portfolio with nonpositive initial cost, nonnegative terminal payoff <almost surely>, and a strict benefit either from a negative initial cost or a positive payoff with positive <probability>. An <equivalent martingale measure> is a <probability> law equivalent to the physical law under which discounted traded prices are martingales. The bond has constant price one, so no discount factor is needed here.

Order the four states as $UU,UD,DU,DD$. Equivalence requires positive <probability> in every state, since the physical product law has full support. Each stock has risk-neutral up <probability> $1/2$, obtained by solving $1=(1+\epsilon)p+(1-\epsilon)(1-p)$. Thus all equivalent <martingale> laws are
$$
\boxed{(q_{UU},q_{UD},q_{DU},q_{DD})=(a,1/2-a,1/2-a,a),\quad0<a<1/2.}
$$
The two risky returns need not be independent under a pricing measure.

A replicating portfolio pays $b+\theta S_2+\varphi S_3$, so its four payoffs satisfy
$$
\boxed{Y_{UU}+Y_{DD}=Y_{UD}+Y_{DU}.}
$$
Conversely this condition suffices: take $\theta=(Y_{UU}-Y_{DU})/(2\epsilon)$, $\varphi=(Y_{UU}-Y_{UD})/(2\epsilon)$, and choose the bond holding to match $Y_{DD}$. The relation then matches the fourth state as well. Its unique price is $(Y_{UU}+Y_{DD})/2=(Y_{UD}+Y_{DU})/2$.

For the same-direction claim, the payoff vector is $(1,0,0,1)$ and its price under $q$ is $2a$. Thus the possible strictly <arbitrage>-free prices fill $(0,1)$ and **the lower <arbitrage> bound is the infimum zero**. It is not achieved by an equivalent measure, and pricing the nonnegative nonzero claim at zero would itself permit <arbitrage> by buying it. Its payoff is not replicable, since the displayed payoff relation fails. A buyer may pay a positive amount for its insurance against joint downward moves, for a view about dependence, or according to risk preferences. Under the stated physical independent law its expected payoff is $13/25$, illustrating that a zero lower bound does not make the payoff economically worthless.

= 29I
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Optimization and Control}

= Solution
{parent=29I}

For fixed terminal time and fixed endpoint, use the maximizing form of the <Pontryagin maximum principle>. For dynamics $\dot q=f(q,u,t)$ and running cost $L$, there are multipliers $(p,p_0)$, not all zero, with $p_0\ge0$, and <Hamiltonian of an optimal-control problem> $\mathscr H=p\cdot f-p_0L$. Along an optimum, $\dot q=\mathscr H_p$, $\dot p=-\mathscr H_q$, and the chosen control maximizes $\mathscr H$ over admissible controls almost everywhere. Fixed terminal state imposes no prescribed terminal value of $p$; fixed time imposes no free-time condition $\mathscr H(T)=0$. The normal case scales $p_0=1$; abnormal extremals have $p_0=0$.

For this problem the normal <Hamiltonian of an optimal-control problem> is
$$
\mathscr H=p_xu+p_yv+k(uy-vx)-\frac12(u^2+v^2),\qquad \dot k=0.
$$
Maximization gives $u=p_x+ky$, $v=p_y-kx$. The <costate> equations give $\dot p_x=kv$, $\dot p_y=-ku$, hence $\dot u=2kv$, $\dot v=-2ku$. Nonzero velocities rotate at constant angular rate, and rotational symmetry permits $u(0)=A$, $v(0)=0$. For a closed path, $2k=2\pi n$, with integer $n\ne0$. Then $z(1)=A^2/(2\pi n)$, so the required positive endpoint has $n>0$, $A^2=2\pi n$ and cost $\pi n$.

To prove a global minimum rather than only classify stationary extremals, write $r=(x,y)$ and subtract its time average; this changes neither its derivative nor $\int(y\dot x-x\dot y)dt$. The resulting closed mean-zero path satisfies the periodic <Wirtinger inequality> $\int|r|^2\le(4\pi^2)^{-1}\int|\dot r|^2$. This follows immediately by its Fourier expansion: each nonzero mode of the derivative has squared multiplier at least $4\pi^2$. The <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> now gives
$$
1=|z(1)|\le\left(\int|r|^2\right)^{1/2}\left(\int|\dot r|^2\right)^{1/2}
\le\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^1(u^2+v^2)dt.
$$
The argument extends to square-integrable controls by the periodic Sobolev/Fourier interpretation. Thus the cost is at least $\pi$ for every feasible control. Equality is attained by
$$
\boxed{u=\sqrt{2\pi}\cos(2\pi t),\quad v=-\sqrt{2\pi}\sin(2\pi t),\quad
x=\frac{\sin(2\pi t)}{\sqrt{2\pi}},\quad y=\frac{\cos(2\pi t)-1}{\sqrt{2\pi}},\quad
z=t-\frac{\sin(2\pi t)}{2\pi}.}
$$
These satisfy every endpoint and have cost $\pi$. Therefore **the minimum is $\pi$**, an <area-constrained quadratic control cost>. The global inequality also prevents an overlooked abnormal candidate from lowering it; in fact an abnormal finite maximizing <Hamiltonian of an optimal-control problem> would force both linear control coefficients to vanish and then force zero velocity, incompatible with $z(1)=1$.

= 30A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Partial Differential Equations}

= i
{parent=30a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the Fourier convention $\widehat f(\xi)=\int_{\mathbb R^3}e^{-ix\cdot\xi}f(x)\,dx$, with inverse $(2\pi)^{-3}\int e^{ix\cdot\xi}\widehat f(\xi)d\xi$. For a <tempered distribution> $T$, define its <Fourier transform> by duality:
$$
\boxed{\langle\widehat T,\phi\rangle=\langle T,\widehat\phi\rangle,\qquad\phi\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^3).}
$$
The <Fourier transform> maps the <Schwartz space> continuously onto itself, making the right side a continuous linear functional there. The use of the same negative exponential in the test-function transform is consistent with bilinear distributional pairing; other unitary or sign conventions change the normalization accordingly.

= ii
{parent=30a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $g\in\mathcal S(\mathbb R^3)$ define <convolution> by
$$
\boxed{(T*g)(x)=\langle T_y,g(x-y)\rangle.}
$$
This is smooth and has at most <polynomial> growth, hence defines a <tempered distribution>. Its equivalent pairing is $\langle T*g,\phi\rangle=\langle T,\widetilde g*\phi\rangle$ with $\widetilde g(y)=g(-y)$. Fourier duality and the usual Schwartz-function <convolution> calculation give
$$
\boxed{\widehat{T*g}=\widehat T\,\widehat g.}
$$
The product on the right is distributional multiplication by the smooth <Schwartz function> $\widehat g$. With the convention in part (i), no extra factor occurs. One can verify it first for functions by changing $x=y+z$ inside the two integrals, then use the displayed pairing to perform the same calculation for arbitrary $T$.

= Solution
{parent=30a}

The following unheaded requests concern the spherical measure, the wave solution and both uniqueness arguments. The missing part (i) above and this root Solution slot restore their coverage while retaining the existing part (ii) anchor.

For $k=|\xi|$, rotational symmetry of the spherical measure gives
$$
\widehat A_t(\xi)=2\pi t^2\int_{-1}^1e^{-itks}\,ds
=\boxed{4\pi t\frac{\sin(tk)}k},
$$
with continuous value $4\pi t^2$ at $k=0$. The spatial <Fourier transform> of the <wave equation> gives $\widehat u_{tt}+k^2\widehat u=0$ with initial data zero and $\widehat g$. Thus $\widehat u=\sin(tk)\widehat g/k$. The <convolution theorem> gives the <Kirchhoff formula>
$$
\boxed{u(t,x)=\frac1{4\pi t}\int_{|y|=t}g(x-y)\,d\Sigma(y)
=\frac t{4\pi}\int_{S^2}g(x+t\omega)\,d\omega.}
$$
It is smooth for Schwartz data, solves the transformed equation, and has $u(0,x)=0$, $u_t(0,x)=g(x)$ by the second expression and its small-time limit.

To prove uniqueness for arbitrary $C^2$ solutions, take their difference $v$, with zero initial data, and set $e=(v_t^2+|\nabla v|^2)/2$, $p=-v_t\nabla v$. Direct differentiation gives $e_t+\nabla\cdot p=v_t(v_{tt}-\Delta v)=0$. For any centre $x_*$ and radius $R$, integrate over the shrinking ball $B(x_*,R-t)$. Differentiating the moving-domain energy gives
$$
\frac d{dt}\int_{B(x_*,R-t)}e
=\int_{\partial B(x_*,R-t)}(v_t\partial_nv-e)\le0,
$$
since $v_t\partial_nv\le(v_t^2+|\nabla v|^2)/2$. Initial energy is zero and energy is nonnegative, so it stays zero. In each such cone $v_t$ and $\nabla v$ vanish, and the initial value is zero, hence $v=0$. Choosing $R>T$ and arbitrary centre proves uniqueness at any time $T$, without imposing finite total energy or decay on the competing solution.

For the equation with the smooth positive time-independent potential $V$, use instead
$$
e_V=\frac12(v_t^2+|\nabla v|^2+Vv^2),\qquad p=-v_t\nabla v.
$$
Then $\partial_te_V+\nabla\cdot p=v_t(v_{tt}-\Delta v+Vv)=0$. The same shrinking-ball estimate is nonpositive because $Vv^2\ge0$. Zero initial data therefore give $v=0$. **The potential-wave initial-value problem has at most one $C^2$ solution**, with no global energy-growth assumption required. Time <independence> of $V$ is what makes this particular local energy identity exact.

= 31E
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Integrable Systems}

= Solution
{parent=31E}

Orient the unit circle counterclockwise and use the standard principal-value Cauchy operator
$$
K\phi(t)=\frac1{\pi i}\operatorname{PV}\int_C\frac{\phi(\tau)}{\tau-t}\,d\tau.
$$
For a Fourier/Laurent mode $t^n$ it gives $+t^n$ when $n\ge0$ and $-t^n$ when $n<0$. This follows from the interior/exterior Cauchy projections, or by direct indentation of the pole. Decompose $\phi=\phi_++\phi_-$ into its nonnegative and negative modes, so $K\phi=\phi_+-\phi_-$. Let
$$
J=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_C(\tau+2\tau^{-1})\phi(\tau)\,d\tau=c_{-2}+2c_0.
$$
The printed equation reduces to
$$
2t\phi_++2t^{-1}\phi_--J(t+t^{-1})=2t^{-1}.
$$
Positive-power coefficients force $\phi_+=J/2$, a constant; powers at most $-2$ force $\phi_-=0$. The $t^{-1}$ coefficient then gives $-J=2$, and the integral definition is consistent with $c_0=-1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\phi(t)=-1\quad(t\in C).}
$$
Direct substitution has $K\phi=-1$, $J=-2$ and gives $2t^{-1}$ as required. The same projection argument on the homogeneous equation forces $J=0$ and both components zero, proving uniqueness in the usual Hölder or $L^2$ class for this singular-integral problem.

= 32D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Principles of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=32D}

Write $|m_1,m_2\rangle$ for product <spin> states, and $J_+=J_{1+}+J_{2+}$. A highest-weight state is killed by $J_+$. For $s=2$ the unique $m=2$ state is $|1,1\rangle$. In the $m=1$ subspace, $J_+$ kills $a|1,0\rangle+b|0,1\rangle$ exactly when $a+b=0$. In the $m=0$ subspace it kills $a|1,-1\rangle+b|0,0\rangle+c|-1,1\rangle$ exactly when $a+b=b+c=0$. Normalizing gives
$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
|2,2\rangle&=|1,1\rangle,\\
|1,1\rangle&=\frac{|1,0\rangle-|0,1\rangle}{\sqrt2},\\
|0,0\rangle&=\frac{|1,-1\rangle-|0,0\rangle+|-1,1\rangle}{\sqrt3}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
The left states are coupled states and the right states are product states. Lowering commutes with particle interchange, so the whole <spin>-$s$ multiplet has the exchange sign of its highest-weight state: $+1$ for $s=0,2$ and $-1$ for $s=1$, or $(-1)^s$. Integer-<spin> identical particles are <bosons>; their spatial partial wave changes by $(-1)^\ell$ under interchange. Symmetry of the full state therefore requires **$\ell+s$ even**.

For decay from total <angular momentum> one, coupling requires $|\ell-s|\le1\le\ell+s$. The case $s=0$ forces $\ell=1$ and violates Bose symmetry. For $s=1$, the allowed range is $\ell=0,1,2$ and symmetry leaves $\ell=1$. For $s=2$, the range is $\ell=1,2,3$ and symmetry leaves $\ell=2$. The identical particles' intrinsic <parity> product is $+1$, so total final <parity> is $(-1)^\ell$. Hence
$$
\boxed{P_X=-1:\ (\ell,s)=(1,1),\qquad
P_X=+1:\ (\ell,s)=(2,2).}
$$
This uses the stated massive-<spin> angular-momentum model; no additional restrictions for massless transverse vector particles have been assumed.

= 33A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Applications of Quantum Mechanics}

= Solution
{parent=33A}

For a self-adjoint <Hamiltonian operator> bounded below, the <Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle> says $E[\psi]=\langle\psi,H\psi\rangle/\langle\psi,\psi\rangle\ge E_0$. Expanding in energy <eigenstates> proves this, since the quotient is a weighted average of energies. Optimize a tractable trial family to obtain an upper bound on the ground energy.

If $H\psi_0=E_0\psi_0$, a regular trial error $\psi=\psi_0+\epsilon\eta$ gives the exact cancellation
$$
E[\psi]-E_0=\frac{\epsilon^2\langle\eta,(H-E_0)\eta\rangle}{\|\psi_0+\epsilon\eta\|^2}=O(\epsilon^2).
$$
The linear terms vanish because $(H-E_0)\psi_0=0$. Here $\eta$ is a fixed finite-energy direction, or is uniformly bounded in the quadratic-form norm. A Hilbert-space norm bound alone is insufficient for an unbounded <Hamiltonian operator>: a norm-$\epsilon$ admixture of an <eigenstate> with energy of order $\epsilon^{-2}$ can have order-one energy error. The usual quadratic-error claim therefore presupposes a controlled-energy perturbation.

For the first excited energy minimize the quotient on states orthogonal to the exact <ground state>. Alternatively diagonalize $H$ in a trial subspace and use the min-max principle: its second Ritz <eigenvalue> is an upper bound for the second exact <eigenvalue>. An arbitrary trial function without either <orthogonality> or a min-max construction need not bound the first excited level.

The one-dimensional even potential has even <ground state> and odd <first excited state>, by the Sturm oscillation ordering. Choose the automatically orthogonal odd trial function $\psi_a(x)=Nx e^{-ax^2/2}$, $a>0$. <Integration by parts> gives the kinetic numerator $\int|\psi_a'|^2$, and the given Gaussian moments give
$$
E_1\le E(a)=\frac{3a}{2}+\frac3{2a}+\frac{15}{4a^2}.
$$
For example, the kinetic factor is $(I_0-2aI_2+a^2I_4)/I_2=3a/2$, while the potential factors are $I_4/I_2$ and $I_6/I_2$. Differentiation gives the unique positive minimizer $a^3-a-5=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{a\approx1.90416,\qquad E_1\le E(a)\approx4.67824.}
$$
This is an upper estimate, not an exact <eigenvalue>. Improve it with odd <polynomial>-Gaussian trial functions such as $(x+bx^3)e^{-ax^2/2}$, or a larger odd Ritz basis, and optimize the width. The <parity> restriction keeps all such states orthogonal to the even <ground state>.

= 34D
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Statistical Physics}

= Solution
{parent=34D}

For a fixed amount of simple compressible material, $dU=T,dS-p,dV$ and $G=U-TS+pV$ gives $dG=-S,dT+V,dp$. Equality of mixed partial derivatives of $G$ therefore yields the <Maxwell relation>
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial p}\right)_T=-\left(\frac{\partial V}{\partial T}\right)_p.}
$$
In the insulated throttling process, piston work into the gas is $p_1V_1$ and work out is $p_2V_2$. Neglecting changes in macroscopic kinetic and gravitational energy, the first law gives $U_2-U_1=p_1V_1-p_2V_2$. Thus **$U_1+p_1V_1=U_2+p_2V_2$**, so <enthalpy> is conserved. The irreversible process is not assumed isentropic.

Since $dH=T,dS+V,dp$ and $dS=(C_p/T)dT-(\partial V/\partial T)_pdp$, one has $dH=C_p,dT+[V-T(\partial V/\partial T)_p]dp$. Therefore the <Joule-Thomson coefficient> is
$$
\boxed{\mu_{JT}=\left(\frac{\partial T}{\partial p}\right)_H
=\frac{T(\partial V/\partial T)_p-V}{C_p}=\frac{V(\alpha T-1)}{C_p}.}
$$
For an <ideal gas>, $V\propto T$ at fixed pressure and $\alpha=1/T$, so **$\mu_{JT}=0$**. A pressure drop has $dp<0$, so cooling, $dT<0$, requires **$\mu_{JT}>0$** in the operating range.

= 35A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=General Relativity}

= Solution
{parent=35A}

The connection is torsion-free, so on a scalar $\nabla_a\nabla_b\phi=\partial_a\partial_b\phi-\Gamma^c_{ab}\partial_c\phi$ is symmetric in $a,b$. A covector also carries a connection acting on its own index, and commuting derivatives gives curvature, generally nonzero.

Use the curvature convention $[\nabla_a,\nabla_b]v_c=R^d{}_{cab}v_d$, consistent with the identity requested in the PDF. Some texts define the opposite Riemann sign, reversing both formulas. Expanding the prescribed derivative combination of the <Killing equation> gives
$$
0=\nabla_aS_{bc}+\nabla_bS_{ac}-\nabla_cS_{ab}
=2\nabla_a\nabla_bv_c-[\nabla_a,\nabla_b]v_c+[\nabla_a,\nabla_c]v_b+[\nabla_b,\nabla_c]v_a.
$$
The three commutator terms are $[-R^d{}_{cab}+R^d{}_{bac}+R^d{}_{abc}]v_d=2R^d{}_{abc}v_d$, by antisymmetry in the last two indices and the first <Bianchi identity>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\nabla_a\nabla_bv_c=-R^d{}_{abc}v_d.}
$$
In flat Cartesian Minkowski coordinates this says $\partial_a\partial_bv_c=0$. Hence all Killing fields are affine:
$$
\boxed{v^a=a^a+\omega^a{}_b x^b,\qquad \omega_{ab}=-\omega_{ba}.}
$$
The constant term gives four translations and the antisymmetric term six Lorentz generators, rotations and boosts. Conversely every such field satisfies the <Killing equation>.

For the static block metric, time derivatives vanish and $g_{0i}=0$, $g^{00}=-1/f$. The Christoffel formula gives $\Gamma^0_{00}=\Gamma^0_{ij}=0$ and $\Gamma^0_{0i}=\Gamma^0_{i0}=(\partial_if)/(2f)$. The vector $v^a=(1,0,0,0)$ has covector components $v_0=-f$, $v_i=0$. Its only potentially nonzero derivatives are $\nabla_iv_0=-\partial_if/2$ and $\nabla_0v_i=\partial_if/2$; spatial-spatial and time-time derivatives vanish. Their symmetric sums are zero, proving **$\partial_t$ is a <Killing vector field>**.

= 36B
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Fluid Dynamics II}

= i
{parent=36b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The outward flux through a spherical cap of radius $R$ inside the cone is
$$
\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\alpha\left(-\frac A{R^2}\right)R^2\sin\theta\,d\theta,d\phi
=-2\pi A(1-\cos\alpha)=-Q.
$$
Thus **the inward extraction flux is $Q$** at leading order. The thin no-slip wall layer is omitted from this approximation; its smaller displacement-flux effect supplies the later correction.

= ii
{parent=36b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Using local speed $A/R^2$ and length $R$, the <Reynolds number> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}(R)=\frac{A}{\nu R}=\frac{Q}{2\pi(1-\cos\alpha)\nu R}.}
$$
Inertia dominates and a thin viscous <boundary layer> is possible when this is large, namely **$R\ll A/\nu$**. The crossover scale is $R\sim Q/[2\pi(1-\cos\alpha)\nu]$, or $Q/\nu$ up to the fixed-angle factor. The inverse dependence on $R$ explains why this inertial sink regime occurs near the tip rather than far away. The fixed cone angle must also allow the wall layer to be small relative to the local cone radius.

= iii
{parent=36b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $x$ increase away from the tip along the wall and $y$ point normally into the fluid. Put $u=u_x$, $v=u_y$ for velocity components and $U(x)=-A/x^2$ for the outer tangential velocity. The local Cartesian momentum and pressure equations are
$$
u\partial_xu+v\partial_yu=-\rho^{-1}p_x+\nu\partial_y^2u=UU'+\nu\partial_y^2u,
\qquad p_y=0.
$$
For a two-dimensional flat layer the usual <continuity equation> is $\partial_xu+\partial_yv=0$. For this axisymmetric cone, however, the circumference is proportional to $x$, so its leading <continuity equation> is
$$
\boxed{\partial_xu+\frac ux+\partial_yv=0,\quad\text{or}\quad\partial_x(xu)+\partial_y(xv)=0.}
$$
The circumference term is of the same order as $\partial_xu$ and cannot be omitted when deriving the cone's similarity equation. Conditions at the stationary wall are $u=v=0$; at the layer edge $u\to U$.

Balance $U^2/x\sim\nu |U|/\delta^2$. With $|U|=A/x^2$ this gives
$$
\boxed{\delta(x)=\sqrt{\frac\nu A}\,x^{3/2},\qquad\frac\delta x=\operatorname{Re}(x)^{-1/2}\ll1.}
$$
The choice of multiplicative constant fixes the normalization of the <similarity variable>. To enforce axisymmetric continuity set $xu=\Psi_y$, $xv=-\Psi_x$, with
$$
\Psi=-\sqrt{A\nu}\,x^{1/2}F(\eta),\qquad\eta=\frac y\delta.
$$
Then
$$
u=-\frac A{x^2}F'(\eta),\qquad
v=\sqrt{A\nu}\,x^{-3/2}\left(\frac12F-\frac32\eta F'\right).
$$
Differentiation gives $u\partial_xu+v\partial_yu=-(A^2/x^5)[2F'^2+FF''/2]$, whereas $UU'=-2A^2/x^5$ and $\nu u_{yy}=-A^2F'''/x^5$. Consequently the <conical sink boundary layer> satisfies
$$
\boxed{F'''-\frac12FF''+2(1-F'^2)=0,\qquad
F(0)=0,\quad F'(0)=0,\quad F'(\infty)=1.}
$$
The first condition is no penetration, the second no slip, and the last matches the inward core flow. If one instead makes a strictly planar approximation and omits the circumference term, the <streamfunction> is $-\sqrt{A\nu}x^{-1/2}F$ and the equation becomes $F'''+FF''/2+2(1-F'^2)=0$. The thickness exponent is the same, but that planar equation is not the axisymmetric cone equation.

Across a cap near $R=x$, the inward flux in a layer truncated at $y=Y$ is, to leading order,
$$
Q_{\mathrm{layer}}(R,Y)=2\pi R\sin\alpha\int_0^Y(-u)\,dy
=2\pi A\sin\alpha\frac{\delta(R)}R F(Y/\delta).
$$
To match to the core, use the finite displacement-flux deficit rather than integrate the uniform outer flow to infinite $Y$. If $I=\int_0^\infty(1-F')d\eta=\lim_{\eta\to\infty}(\eta-F)$, then
$$
\Delta Q=2\pi A\sin\alpha\frac{\delta(R)}R I
=2\pi\sin\alpha\,I\sqrt{A\nu}\,R^{1/2}.
$$
Conservation of the fixed total extraction rate requires an extra core flux of this order. Dividing by cap area, proportional to $R^2$, proves
$$
\boxed{u_{R,\mathrm{outer}}=-\frac A{R^2}+O(\sqrt{A\nu}\,R^{-3/2}).}
$$
The correction is a relative $O(\sqrt{\nu R/A})$ contribution. Its angular structure requires outer matching; the requested radial power follows from the displacement flux alone. Its cap-average direction increases the inward core speed to compensate for the wall deficit.

= 37C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Waves}

= Solution
{parent=37C}

For an isentropic <perfect gas>, $p=K\rho^\gamma$ and $c^2=dp/d\rho$. The one-dimensional <Euler equations> give $D\rho=-\rho u_x$ and $Du=-c^2\rho_x/\rho$, where $D=\partial_t+u\partial_x$. Since $dc/d\rho=(\gamma-1)c/(2\rho)$, these become
$$
Du=-\frac{2c}{\gamma-1}c_x,\qquad Dc=-\frac{\gamma-1}{2}c u_x.
$$
Adding the appropriate $\pm c\partial_x$ terms shows
$$
\boxed{(\partial_t+(u\pm c)\partial_x)\left[u\pm\frac{2(c-c_0)}{\gamma-1}\right]=0.}
$$
These are the <Riemann invariants>. A <simple wave> is a solution in which one invariant is spatially and temporally constant, so all local state variables depend on the other one. For a right-moving wave connected to rest, the minus invariant is zero and $c=c_0+(\gamma-1)u/2$. The plus characteristic then has speed $u+c=c_0+(\gamma+1)u/2$, giving
$$
\boxed{u_t+\left(c_0+\frac{\gamma+1}{2}u\right)u_x=0.}
$$
For the supplied damped equation put $b=(\gamma+1)/2$. Along $\dot x=c_0+bu$, $\dot u=-\alpha u$, so an initial label $x_0$ gives
$$
\boxed{u=v(x_0)e^{-\alpha t},\qquad
x-c_0t=x_0+\frac b\alpha(1-e^{-\alpha t})v(x_0).}
$$
This solves the initial-value problem while the label map remains invertible. Its derivative is $1+(b/\alpha)(1-e^{-\alpha t})v'(x_0)$. Nonnegative slopes cannot cause crossing; for negative slopes, the condition $\alpha>b\sup_{v'<0}|v'|$ keeps that derivative strictly positive. Hence **the stated damping condition prevents <shock> formation at every finite time**. Equality also avoids a finite-time crossing, although the derivative may tend to zero as time tends to infinity. The strict inequality in the question is sufficient rather than necessary.

= 38C
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Numerical Analysis}

= a
{parent=38c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

One form of the <Householder-John theorem> is as follows. Suppose $A=A^*$ and $A=M-N$, with $M$ invertible and $W=M+M^*-A$ positive definite. Then the stationary iteration $Mx^{(k+1)}=Nx^{(k)}+b$ converges for every initial vector exactly when $A$ is positive definite; its iteration matrix is $B=M^{-1}N$ and convergence means <spectral radius> below one.

For a known positive definite system, design a splitting with $M$ easy to solve, such as diagonal or triangular, and arrange $M+M^*-A>0$. The theorem then guarantees convergence. For example $M=sI$ has $W=2sI-A>0$ when $2s$ exceeds the largest <eigenvalue> of $A$. In the converse direction, the discrete energy identity $A-B^*AB=(I-B)^*W(I-B)$ explains the criterion: stable $B$ gives $A$ as a convergent sum of positive matrices, while positive $A,W$ force every iteration <eigenvalue> inside the unit disk.

= b
{parent=38c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Divide the supplied scheme by $\omega$ and take
$$
M=\frac D\omega+L,\qquad N=\left(\frac1\omega-1\right)D-U.
$$
Then $A=M-N$, and $M$ is invertible because it is lower triangular with positive diagonal $D/\omega$. Symmetry gives $U=L^T$, so
$$
\boxed{W=M+M^T-A=\left(\frac2\omega-1\right)D>0\quad(0<\omega<2).}
$$
The <Householder-John theorem> therefore gives convergence.

For a direct spectral proof, let $B=M^{-1}N=I-M^{-1}A$. Expansion verifies $A-B^*AB=(I-B)^*W(I-B)$. If $Bv=\lambda v$ with $v\ne0$, then
$$
(1-|\lambda|^2)v^*Av=|1-\lambda|^2v^*Wv.
$$
The value $\lambda=1$ is impossible since $(I-B)v=M^{-1}Av$ and $A$ is invertible. Both quadratic forms are positive, hence $|\lambda|<1$. The error obeys $e^{(k+1)}=Be^{(k)}$ and tends to zero, proving **convergence to the unique solution $A^{-1}b$ for every initial iterate**. This is the successive-over-relaxation iteration, with no assertion of convergence at the excluded endpoints.
